---
title: How to store and handle coins before you send them for grading
date: 2026-11-04
description: 'Hold coins by the edges, avoid PVC flips and paper envelopes, keep them cool and dry, and never clean them: simple habits that protect a coin before it is graded.'
draft: false
---
A coin's grade is a record of its condition on the day it is examined. Everything that happens to it before then, from the way it is picked up to the holder it sits in, can help preserve that condition or quietly wear it away. The good news is that protecting a coin takes very little: a few careful habits and the right materials.

## Handling: the edges, and nothing else

Fingers carry oils, salts and moisture. Touch the face of a coin and you can leave a fingerprint that, over months or years, etches into the surface and becomes permanent. On a coin with mint luster or proof surfaces, a single print can lower the grade.

- **Hold the coin by its edge**, between your thumb and forefinger. Never touch the obverse or reverse.
- **Work over something soft**, such as a folded towel or a velvet pad, so that a dropped coin lands safely. A coin that hits a hard table or floor can pick up a rim nick or a mark in the field in an instant.
- **Consider gloves.** Clean, powder-free nitrile gloves keep skin oils off the coin and still let you grip it firmly. Cotton gloves are also used, but they can make small coins slippery and may carry lint or dust. Whatever you use, still hold the coin by its edge.
- **Don't talk over an exposed coin.** It sounds fussy, but tiny droplets from breath and speech can land on the surface and later show up as small spots, especially on copper and on proofs.
- **Keep handling to a minimum.** Every time a coin comes out of its holder, it is exposed to risk. Look at it in the holder whenever you can.

## Storage: choose inert materials

What a coin is stored in matters as much as how it is handled. Some common materials react with metal over time.

### Avoid PVC flips

Soft, flexible plastic flips are often made with PVC (polyvinyl chloride). As the plastic ages, the chemicals that keep it soft can migrate onto the coin and leave a greenish, sticky film. Left long enough, the residue can damage the surface underneath. If a flip feels soft and pliable, and especially if it has a slightly oily feel or smell, it is likely to contain PVC.

Better choices are flips made of inert polyester film (often sold as Mylar) or other archival-quality, PVC-free holders. They are stiffer and less pleasant to handle, but they will not attack the coin.

### Be careful with paper and cardboard

Ordinary paper envelopes and cardboard can contain sulfur and other compounds that tone or tarnish coins, sometimes unevenly. Cardboard 2x2 holders are widely used, but the staples that close them can scratch a coin or a neighboring holder if they are pushed in or pulled out carelessly. If you use them, flatten the staples completely and remove them with care.

### Other things to avoid

- Rubber bands and household plastic bags, which can contain sulfur or plasticizers.
- Loose coins rattling together in a jar, a box or a pouch. Coin-to-coin contact is how bag marks happen.
- Tape of any kind on or touching the coin. Adhesive leaves a residue that can be very difficult to remove safely.
- Old albums with slides that rub across the faces of the coins each time they are moved.

## Environment: cool, dry and steady

Humidity and temperature swings are slow enemies. Moisture speeds up corrosion and spotting, particularly on copper, and changes in temperature can cause condensation inside holders.

- Keep coins somewhere with a stable temperature and moderate humidity: an interior room or closet is usually better than an attic, a basement or a garage.
- Store them in a closed container with a fresh pack of silica gel, and replace or recharge the gel regularly.
- Keep coins away from wood that has been freshly varnished or treated, from fresh paint and from household cleaning products, all of which can give off fumes.
- If you use a safe or safe deposit box, the same rules apply: inert holders, a desiccant and no loose coins.

## Don't clean, don't "improve"

It can be tempting to brighten a coin before sending it in. Please don't. Cleaning leaves marks that cannot be undone and often turns a coin that could have received a numeric grade into one described with a details grade instead. Our article on [how to spot a cleaned coin](/blog/how-to-spot-a-cleaned-coin) explains what cleaning does to a surface and why it is so easy to see. Natural, original toning is part of a coin's history, and collectors generally prefer it left alone.

## Getting ready to send

When you are ready to submit, a little preparation makes the whole process smoother:

- **Make a list** of each coin with a short description, so the contents of your package can be checked against your submission when it arrives.
- **Photograph each coin** while it is still in your holder, for your own records.
- **Keep each coin in its own inert holder or flip**, and never tape a coin directly. Our [submissions page](/submit) explains how to pack and ship securely.
- **Ask if you are unsure.** If you don't know whether a coin is worth grading, or which service suits it, tell us before you send it.

## After grading

Once a coin is graded, it is sealed in a tamper-evident holder that protects it from handling while keeping both sides in view. You can read about how it is made on our page about [the holder and label](/holder). Even sealed coins benefit from the same cool, dry storage, and you can always check a coin's record by entering its certificate number on our [verify a certificate](/verify) page.

Good habits cost almost nothing, and they protect the one thing a grade can never restore: an original, undamaged surface.
